description:
MCP Connecting Robinhood

List the user's brokerage accounts. Each account includes an agentic_allowed field indicating whether it's tradable by you — accounts where it's false are read-only to you. Use this to look up account_number values needed by other tools. Exactly one account is tradable by you; when the user is choosing an account for a trade, use that account directly without asking. Does NOT return reliable buying power — route buying-power questions through get_portfolio. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_accounts(args: {}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // The user's brokerage accounts. Empty when the user has none.
  accounts: null | Array<null | {
  // Brokerage account number; pass to any tool that requires account_number. Mask all but the last 4 digits (e.g. '••••1234') when displaying to the user.
  account_number: string;
  // Affiliate / region code (e.g. 'RHF', 'RHUK', 'RHSG'). Affects feature availability for the account.
  affiliate?: string;
  // True when this account is accessible to this agent specifically. An account with false may be agentic-enabled but scoped to a different agent type — do not tell the user it is disabled. Sole source of truth for eligibility — never infer it from nickname text. review_*/place_*/cancel_* tools reject accounts where this is false.
  agentic_allowed: boolean;
  // Account type, e.g. 'individual', 'ira_traditional', 'ira_roth', 'ira_traditional_inherited', 'ira_roth_inherited', 'isa_stocks_and_shares'.
  brokerage_account_type: string;
  // True when the account is currently deactivated.
  deactivated: boolean;
  // True for the user's default account. Surface when choosing among multiple accounts.
  is_default: boolean;
  // Management classification (e.g. 'managed') when applicable; layered on top of brokerage_account_type rather than replacing it.
  management_type?: string;
  // User-set nickname; present alongside account_number when set. Cosmetic only — not an agentic_allowed or agentic-account signal.
  nickname?: string;
  // Options approval: 'option_level_2' (long calls/puts, covered calls, cash-secured puts), 'option_level_3' (spreads and complex strategies), or empty/'option_level_0' (not approved).
  option_level: string;
  // True when the account is permanently closed (cannot be reactivated).
  permanently_deactivated: boolean;
  // Crypto account number for this account (e.g. 'RHC1234567'). Absent when no linked crypto account exists. Mask all but the last 4 digits (e.g. '••••1234') when displaying to the user.
  rhc_account_number?: string;
  // Numeric brokerage account number — pass this (not account_number) to any nummus-backed tool that asks for an account identifier.
  rhs_account_number: string;
  // Account state — 'active' is normal; anything else (deactivated, restricted, etc.) means the account cannot trade normally.
  state: string;
  // 'cash', 'margin', or 'limited_margin' — the trading-permissions classification for the brokerage account. 'limited_margin' (instant settlement without full margin leverage) applies to accounts where full margin is unavailable, such as retirement (IRA), managed, and agentic accounts. Crypto accounts are never marginable, so a linked crypto account has no equivalent.
  type: string;
  // Proceeds from recent sales not yet settled (cash accounts settle T+1), in the account's currency. Informational only and may lag live trading — this is NOT a spendable or order-gating figure; buying_power from get_portfolio is authoritative for what can be traded now. A cash-type account with unsettled_funds greater than 0 could trade with those proceeds immediately by upgrading to limited margin. Absent when upstream does not report it — treat as unknown, not zero.
  unsettled_funds?: string;
  // The caller's own options approval on a multi-member (trust) account: 'option_level_2', 'option_level_3', or absent. Absent on single-owner and joint accounts and whenever the caller holds no level of their own. When present and below option_level the caller is view-only for options on this account and cannot place, replace, cancel or exercise; treat user_option_level, not option_level, as their tradable level.
  user_option_level?: string;
}>;
};
  // Sort the list deterministically when presenting: the default account first, agentic accounts (agentic_allowed=true) second, then other individual accounts, then retirement (IRA) accounts. Present account_number, rhc_account_number (label it "Crypto Account"), brokerage_account_type (label it "Account Type" — a linked crypto account always shares it, so never add a separate crypto account type column), type (label it "Brokerage Trading Type"), nickname (if set), and is_default — in that column order. In the Crypto Account column show "N/A" for accounts with an empty rhc_account_number; omit the column entirely only when no account has one. When referring to the account in user-facing prose (e.g. "your <X> account"), use nickname when set; fall back to brokerage_account_type otherwise. When displaying account_number or rhc_account_number to the user, mask all but the last 4 digits (e.g. '••••1234'); pass the full value to other tools unchanged — including get_option_level_upgrade_info and any fallback upgrade URL; masking breaks the link. Flag agentic_allowed when the user is choosing an account for a trade. There is exactly one agentic_allowed=true account — use it directly rather than asking which account to trade. agentic_allowed is caller-relative — true means accessible to this agent, false means this agent cannot act on it (the account may still be agentic-enabled for a different agent). Never state agentic_allowed's field name or raw boolean value to the user. Do not use nickname to determine agentic-account eligibility. Skip false accounts for trade operations; if the user asks why, say the account is not accessible to this agent, not that it needs to be enabled. When user_option_level is present (multi-member trust accounts), it is this user's own options level on that account and is the level to reason about for options actions there; if it is below option_level the user is view-only for options on that account. When user_option_level is absent on a trust account (brokerage_account_type='trust_revocable'), the user holds no options level of their own there — do not offer options actions on that account, even though option_level is set. When option_level is empty or 'option_level_0' and the user wants options, call get_option_level_upgrade_info with the account_number; if that tool is unavailable, direct the user to https://applink.robinhood.com/upgrade_options?account_number=<that account's account_number> (embed the full unmasked account_number in the URL). management_type='managed' is layered on brokerage_account_type — surface alongside, not as a replacement. For tools that ask for an account identifier on a crypto-backed flow (get_crypto_positions, future crypto write tools), pass rhs_account_number; for everything else (equities, options, etc.) pass account_number. Do not add an unsettled_funds column to the account table — surface it only via the following rule. Whenever you report an account's funds, value, or buying power and that account has type='cash' with unsettled_funds greater than 0, and the user wants to access unsettled funds or other features available in a limited margin account — if the get_limited_margin_upgrade_info tool is available — call it with the account_number and share the appropriate upgrade link. If that tool is not available, say nothing about limited margin (there is no fallback).
  guide: string;
}>>; };
```

MCP Connecting Robinhood

Read the log of fired alerts — what fired, when, and at what price or indicator value — with read/unread state. Poll this to learn whether any alert has fired; alert events are not pushed to the agent. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_alert_log(args: {
  // Optional: equity or crypto, limiting the log to one asset class. Omitted = all classes.
  asset_class?: string;
  // Opaque pagination token from a prior response's next_cursor. Omit for the first page. When passing a cursor, repeat the same asset_class, since, and limit as the call that returned it — the token's shape depends on which index served that page, and changing filters between pages causes an upstream error.
  cursor?: string;
  // Max events per page, 1-100. Omitted = 20. Raise it (e.g. to 100) to collect a full mark_alerts_read batch in one page.
  limit?: number;
  // Optional RFC3339 timestamp: only events triggered at or after this instant. Omitted = the full retention window.
  since?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // Fired-alert events, most recent first. Empty when nothing has fired in the requested window.
  events: null | Array<{
  // The alert setting that fired — the same alert_id get_alerts returns; use it to correlate with update_alert/delete_alert. Absent when the upstream did not record it.
  alert_id?: string;
  // Opaque identifier for this fired-alert event. Pass to mark_alerts_read; never construct one. This is NOT the alert setting's alert_id.
  alert_log_id: string;
  // 'equity' or 'crypto'.
  asset_class: string;
  // Which condition fired — same vocabulary as get_alerts, e.g. price_above, sma_crosses, price_above_boll_upper.
  condition_type: string;
  // Whether this event has been marked read (handled). false = not yet handled by any client, including this agent.
  read: boolean;
  // Ticker symbol. Bare symbol for equities (e.g. AAPL). For crypto this is the base currency code (e.g. BTC), not a trading pair — never BTC-USD.
  symbol: string;
  // The configured level and the market values at the moment the condition was met — all as of triggered_at.
  trigger: {
  // up when the watched value crossed above the level, down when below. Absent if the upstream did not record it.
  direction?: string;
  // The indicator configuration that was active when the alert fired. Present for indicator conditions.
  indicator?: null | {
  // macd only: fast EMA period.
  fast_period?: number;
  // Bar size in seconds.
  interval_secs?: number;
  // Which indicator family this configuration is for: sma, ema, vwap, rsi, macd, or boll.
  kind: string;
  // boll only: which moving average the bands are built on, sma or ema.
  ma_type?: string;
  // Number of bars in the indicator's lookback window. Not set for vwap.
  period?: number;
  // macd only: signal-line EMA period.
  signal_period?: number;
  // macd only: slow EMA period.
  slow_period?: number;
  // boll only: number of standard deviations for the bands.
  std_dev?: number;
};
  // Decimal string. The indicator line's value at the moment price crossed it. Set for price-vs-indicator-line conditions (e.g. price_above_sma, macd_above_signal).
  indicator_value?: string;
  // Decimal string. The configured trigger price. Set for price conditions (price_above/price_below/price_crosses).
  target_price?: string;
  // Decimal string. The configured target indicator value. Set for indicator-value conditions (e.g. sma_above, rsi_below).
  target_value?: string;
  // Decimal string. The traded price at the moment the condition was met — as of triggered_at, NOT the current market price. Set for price conditions and price-vs-indicator-line conditions.
  triggered_price?: string;
  // Decimal string. The indicator's value at the moment the condition was met. Set for indicator-value conditions.
  triggered_value?: string;
};
  // RFC3339 traded time when the condition was met — NOT when any notification was delivered, and not current market time.
  triggered_at: string;
}>;
  // Pass as cursor to fetch the next page. Absent on the last page.
  next_cursor?: string;
  // The user's total unread fired-alert count across all pages and symbols, capped at 100.
  total_unread_count: number;
};
  // Fired alerts are returned most-recent-first. triggered_at and the trigger values describe the moment the condition was met, not the current market — phrase them as "as of <triggered_at>" and call get_equity_quotes for equities or get_crypto_quotes for crypto for the price now (get_crypto_quotes takes a pair symbol: construct it as the symbol + "-USD" for USD-quoted pairs, BTC -> BTC-USD, only when the symbol has no hyphen already since it can arrive pre-formed as a pair, or look up a non-USD-quoted pair via get_currency_pairs). An empty log means nothing has fired, which is a valid result rather than an error. read=false marks events not yet handled; after relaying them to the user, call mark_alerts_read with those alert_log_ids (never alert_id) so the same event is not reported twice. This log is the only way to learn an alert fired — alert events are not pushed to the agent, so never tell the user you will watch the market or notify them; they still receive their usual Robinhood notifications.
  guide: string;
}>>; };
```

MCP Connecting Robinhood

List the price/indicator alerts the user currently has configured (equities and crypto only), optionally filtered to one symbol. Use to look up alert_id values for update_alert/delete_alert, or to check what's currently being watched. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_alerts(args: {
  // Only alongside symbol: equity or crypto, pinning which asset the symbol names. When omitted the symbol is resolved as an equity first, then as crypto. Cannot be used without symbol.
  asset_class?: string;
  // Pagination cursor from a previous response's next_cursor. Omit for the first page.
  cursor?: string;
  // Filter to one asset's alerts, e.g. AAPL or BTC. Omit for all alerts.
  symbol?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // Only present when filtering by symbol: true when that asset is already at its per-asset alert cap, so creating another alert on it would be rejected. Absent on unfiltered calls — absence does not mean there is headroom.
  alert_limit_reached?: null | boolean;
  // The user's configured alerts. Empty when the user has none set.
  alerts: null | Array<{
  // Opaque identifier for this alert. Pass to update_alert/delete_alert; never construct one.
  alert_id: string;
  // 'equity' or 'crypto'.
  asset_class: string;
  // The configured threshold/indicator details for condition_type.
  condition: {
  // Indicator configuration. Present whenever condition_type names a technical indicator.
  indicator?: null | {
  // macd only: fast EMA period.
  fast_period?: number;
  // Bar size in seconds.
  interval_secs?: number;
  // Which indicator family this configuration is for: sma, ema, vwap, rsi, macd, or boll.
  kind: string;
  // boll only: which moving average the bands are built on, sma or ema.
  ma_type?: string;
  // Number of bars in the indicator's lookback window. Not set for vwap.
  period?: number;
  // macd only: signal-line EMA period.
  signal_period?: number;
  // macd only: slow EMA period.
  slow_period?: number;
  // boll only: number of standard deviations for the bands.
  std_dev?: number;
};
  // Decimal string. Set for plain price-threshold conditions (condition_type price_above/price_below/price_crosses).
  target_price?: string;
  // Decimal string. Set for plain indicator-value conditions (e.g. condition_type sma_above, rsi_below).
  target_value?: string;
};
  // Which condition this alert uses, e.g. price_above, price_below, price_crosses, sma_above, rsi_below, price_crosses_sma, macd_above_signal. Labels derive from the upstream setting type, so these are examples, not the full set — new condition types may appear beyond them.
  condition_type: string;
  // RFC3339 creation time.
  created_at: string;
  // Human-readable instrument name, e.g. 'Robinhood Markets'.
  display_name: string;
  // Whether this alert is on. When false it will not fire until re-enabled; it has not been deleted.
  enabled: boolean;
  // Ticker symbol. Bare symbol for equities (e.g. AAPL). For crypto this is the base currency code (e.g. BTC), not a trading pair — never BTC-USD.
  symbol: string;
  // RFC3339 last-updated time.
  updated_at: string;
}>;
  // Pagination cursor for the next page. Omitted when there are no more alerts.
  next_cursor?: string;
};
  // Alerts are returned grouped by symbol (alphabetically), newest-created first within each symbol. An empty alerts list means the user has no alerts set — a valid result, not an error. Pass alert_id to update_alert or delete_alert; never construct one. Equity alerts carry a bare symbol (AAPL) and crypto alerts the base currency code (BTC, never BTC-USD) — route to get_equity_quotes for equities or get_crypto_quotes for crypto when the user asks how close an alert is to firing, since no live price is included here; get_crypto_quotes takes a pair symbol, so construct it as the symbol + "-USD" for USD-quoted pairs (BTC -> BTC-USD) — append "-USD" only when the symbol has no hyphen already, since it can arrive pre-formed as a pair — or look up a non-USD-quoted pair via get_currency_pairs. Surface enabled=false before telling the user an alert is watching the market.
  guide: string;
}>>; };
```

MCP Connecting Robinhood

List crypto order history for an account — newest first. Open and closed orders, including fills, cancellations, and rejections. Pass order_id to retrieve a single order by UUID.

Filtering tips:
- Prefer narrow queries: combine state (or state_group), symbol, and/or created_at_gte for specific questions.
- created_at_gte: interpret relative times in the user's timezone, convert to UTC before sending.
- updated_at_gte: useful when polling for order fills or state transitions.
- symbol triggers a symbol→currency_pair_id lookup; omit it if you don't need it.

When the user asks broadly for "orders" or "my orders" without naming an asset class or order type, call get_equity_orders, get_option_orders, get_crypto_orders, and get_advanced_orders in parallel so OCO groups are not omitted.

For requests spanning all accounts, re-fetch the account list with get_accounts first — a list from earlier in the conversation may be missing newly created accounts. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_crypto_orders(args: {
  // Lower bound (inclusive) on created_at. ISO 8601 UTC; naive values are interpreted as UTC.
  created_at_gte?: string;
  // Pagination cursor. Omit for the first page; for the next page, pass the prior response's next value back verbatim.
  cursor?: string;
  // Filter to a single order by UUID. The response shape is unchanged (results[] with at most one entry); empty when the order does not belong to the account.
  order_id?: string;
  // Numeric brokerage account number (the 'rhs_account_number' field on get_accounts entries — distinct from the alphanumeric 'account_number'). Required.
  rhs_account_number: string;
  // Filter by side: 'buy' or 'sell'.
  side?: string;
  // Filter by single state: queued, confirmed, partially_filled, filled, canceled, rejected, failed, voided. For open-vs-closed shortcuts, use state_group instead.
  state?: string;
  // Filter by state group: 'open' or 'closed'. Quick shortcut — 'open' covers queued/confirmed/partially_filled, 'closed' covers filled/canceled/rejected/failed/voided. Mutually exclusive with state.
  state_group?: string;
  // Filter by crypto symbol (e.g. 'BTC', 'ETH', or 'BTC-USD'). Triggers a symbol→currency_pair_id lookup before the orders call.
  symbol?: string;
  // Lower bound (inclusive) on updated_at. ISO 8601 UTC. Useful for polling for fills — each state transition bumps updated_at.
  updated_at_gte?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

MCP Connecting Robinhood

List open crypto positions for a specific brokerage account. Returns asset, quantity, transferable amount, and cost-basis breakdown. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_crypto_positions(args: {
  // Pagination cursor. Omit for the first page; for the next page, pass the prior response's next value back verbatim.
  cursor?: string;
  // Numeric brokerage account number (the 'rhs_account_number' field on get_accounts entries — distinct from the alphanumeric 'account_number'). Required. Do NOT pass rhc_account_number (the linked crypto account, labeled 'Crypto Account' on get_accounts) — that is a different identifier space and will return no positions.
  rhs_account_number: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // Opaque cursor for the next page — NOT a URL. When non-empty more pages exist: call this tool again with cursor set to this value verbatim, leaving the other arguments unchanged. Empty on the last page.
  next?: string;
  // Open crypto positions on this page. Empty when the cursor has no rows.
  results: null | Array<null | {
  // Brokerage account UUID this position belongs to.
  account_id: string;
  // Cost basis for average buy price and P&L, in USD; normally one entry, absent when no cost basis is known. If more than one entry is present, report each separately and do not add direct_cost_basis across entries.
  cost_bases?: null | Array<null | {
  // Total cost basis for direct_quantity, in USD. Zero means the basis for these units is not known, not that they were free.
  direct_cost_basis: string;
  // Quantity covered by direct_cost_basis.
  direct_quantity: string;
  // Total cost basis for intraday_quantity.
  intraday_cost_basis: string;
  // Quantity acquired today and still held.
  intraday_quantity: string;
}>;
  // Asset currency this position holds. Use currency.code as the asset symbol.
  currency: null | {
  // Currency ticker (e.g. 'BTC', 'ETH', 'USD').
  code: string;
  // Full currency name (e.g. 'Bitcoin', 'Ethereum').
  name: string;
  // 'cryptocurrency' for crypto assets, 'fiat' for cash currencies.
  type: string;
};
  // UUID of the canonical pair quoted against USD (e.g. BTC-USD). Pass in the currency_pair_ids array to add_to_watchlist / remove_from_watchlist. For current price call get_crypto_quotes with the pair SYMBOL (construct as currency.code + "-USD"), not this UUID. Empty when no such pair exists for this asset (rare).
  currency_pair_id?: string;
  // Total quantity held in asset units (e.g. BTC), net of pending withdrawal holds only. For the amount the user can actually sell right now, use 'quantity_transferable'.
  quantity: string;
  // Quantity locked by open outgoing sell orders.
  quantity_held_for_sell: string;
  // Quantity available to sell or transfer right now — net of open sell orders, pending withdrawals, quarantine, and any other holds. This is the correct field for 'how much can I sell?', including for crypto purchased on instant funds. Strictly ≤ 'quantity'.
  quantity_transferable: string;
}>;
};
  // Sellable quantity: use `quantity_transferable`, not `quantity` — `quantity_transferable` is the amount available to sell right now (already nets out open sell orders, pending withdrawals, holds, quarantine, and instant-funds restrictions); `quantity` only subtracts pending withdrawals. The two are equal in the common case but diverge when there are open sells or unsettled instant buys. Asset name comes from currency.code; for current price, call get_crypto_quotes with the pair symbol (construct as `currency.code + "-USD"` for USD-quoted pairs, or look up via get_currency_pairs) — note that get_crypto_quotes takes the symbol, NOT currency_pair_id. Per-unit average buy price for an entry is direct_cost_basis / direct_quantity; when direct_cost_basis is 0, say the basis is unknown rather than reporting a $0 average. direct_quantity is in asset units, so it is safe to sum across entries. The total can differ from the holding's quantity in either direction; report the difference without asserting a cause (possible reasons include transfers or rewards with no captured cost basis, a pending withdrawal, a recent sell still settling, or lot records still being reconciled). When the total is less than quantity, the average buy price covers only that subset of the units. The intraday_* fields isolate today's still-held portion when the user asks about today specifically; they can lag a very recent fill. Suppress cost_bases when not asked. No market price here — for current value or P&L, multiply the get_crypto_quotes result by quantity.
  guide: string;
}>>; };
```

MCP Connecting Robinhood

Get real-time bid/ask/mark prices plus the previous close for one or more crypto pair symbols (e.g. BTC-USD). This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_crypto_quotes(args: {
  // Optional numeric brokerage account number (the 'rhs_account_number' from get_accounts). When supplied, bid/ask/mark and the previous close are priced on this account's execution routing — the venues its orders actually fill at — and the applied routing is echoed in routing. When omitted, prices use the caller's own routing (it is set at the user level, so any of their crypto accounts yields the same routing). A caller with no crypto account, or an account that cannot be resolved, receives market-maker-routed pricing. Pairs that only trade on market-maker routing are always priced on it, whatever the account's routing, so routing can differ between results in one response.
  rhs_account_number?: string;
  // One or more crypto pair symbols (e.g. 'BTC-USD', 'ETH-USD'). Hyphenated and unhyphenated forms are both accepted on input; the response symbol field comes back unhyphenated.
  symbols: null | Array<string>;
  // Optional IANA timezone name (e.g. 'America/New_York', 'America/Los_Angeles') that anchors the previous-close (open_price) day boundary to the user's local midnight and sets the timezone the response timestamps (bid_time, ask_time, updated_at) are rendered in. Pass ONLY when the user's timezone is known from context — do NOT guess it, infer it from language, or default it. When omitted, the previous close is anchored to midnight US Eastern and timestamps are rendered in US Eastern. Must be a valid IANA name — not an abbreviation ('PST'), a UTC offset ('-08:00'), or 'Local'.
  timezone?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // One entry per requested symbol with live market data. Empty if no symbol resolved.
  results: null | Array<null | {
  // Lowest current sell-order price. Zero when the book is closed or unavailable.
  ask_price: string;
  // Timestamp of ask_price, rendered in the request timezone (the get_crypto_quotes 'timezone' parameter; US Eastern when not supplied). Present when the upstream included side timestamps; otherwise absent.
  ask_time?: null | string;
  // Highest current buy-order price. Zero when the book is closed or unavailable — do NOT present zero as a price.
  bid_price: string;
  // Timestamp of bid_price, rendered in the request timezone (the get_crypto_quotes 'timezone' parameter; US Eastern when not supplied). Present when the upstream included side timestamps; otherwise absent.
  bid_time?: null | string;
  // Currency-pair instrument UUID. Pass in the currency_pair_ids array to add_to_watchlist / remove_from_watchlist — no separate get_currency_pairs call needed just to get the UUID. Also matches get_currency_pairs.id and the currency_pair_id on get_crypto_positions rows.
  id: string;
  // Reference midpoint computed from bid and ask. Zero when both sides of the book are unavailable.
  mark_price: string;
  // Previous close — the reference midpoint (same bid/ask-mid basis as mark_price) at the midnight day boundary of the request timezone (the get_crypto_quotes 'timezone' parameter; US Eastern when not supplied). Crypto trades continuously, so this boundary price is the prior day's close. Resets daily; NOT a last-trade price.
  open_price: string;
  // Execution routing these prices are quoted on, matching the routing on get_crypto_orders rows. bid_price, ask_price, mark_price, and open_price are all priced on this routing. Absent when routing information is unavailable.
  routing?: string;
  // Crypto pair symbol returned by upstream in unhyphenated form (e.g. 'BTCUSD'). To cross-reference with get_currency_pairs.symbol (which is hyphenated, e.g. 'BTC-USD'), strip the hyphen from that field or insert one before the last 3 characters of this one.
  symbol: string;
  // Time this snapshot was last refreshed, rendered in the request timezone (the get_crypto_quotes 'timezone' parameter; US Eastern when not supplied) — the same zone that anchors open_price. Use to judge freshness — when stale, phrase as 'as of <time>' rather than 'current'.
  updated_at: string;
}>;
};
