Get the account's portfolio market value breakdown by asset type and buying power. Use for "how much is my account worth?", "what's my portfolio breakdown?", "how much do I have in options?", and "how much can I spend / afford?" questions. This tool is part of plugin `Robinhood MCP`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__robinhood_get_portfolio(args: {
  // Brokerage account number. Obtain from get_accounts.
  account_number: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{
  // Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.
  data: {
  // Real-time buying power from the broker.
  buying_power: null | {
  // Authoritative spendable figure used to gate order placement. For margin-enabled accounts it already includes available margin leverage; for cash and leverage-disabled accounts it equals the cash-only figure.
  buying_power: string;
  // Currency of the figures, e.g. 'USD'.
  display_currency: string;
  // Buying power for intraday leverage, when applicable; null when the account does not have intraday margin enabled.
  intraday_buying_power?: null | string;
  // Buying power outside the intraday-leverage window; null when not applicable.
  off_intraday_buying_power?: null | string;
  // Buying power ignoring margin leverage. Typically <= buying_power. Negative when the account is in deficit — new positions cannot be opened until the deficit is cleared.
  unleveraged_buying_power: string;
};
  // Available cash (includes early access from pending deposits).
  cash: string;
  // Spendable buying power for crypto buys. Cash-only (crypto is not marginable), so it excludes the equity margin leverage that buying_power may include — quote this for 'how much can I spend on crypto'. Omitted when unavailable.
  crypto_buying_power?: null | {
  // Authoritative spendable figure for crypto buys (cash-only; excludes equity margin leverage).
  buying_power: string;
};
  // Value of crypto holdings.
  crypto_value: string;
  // Currency all values are denominated in (e.g. USD).
  currency: string;
  // Value of stock and ETF positions.
  equity_value: string;
  // Value of event contract positions.
  event_contracts_value: string;
  // Value of fixed income instruments.
  fixed_income_value: string;
  // Value of futures positions (includes RHD cost basis).
  futures_value: string;
  // Value of mutual fund positions.
  mutual_funds_value: string;
  // Value of options positions.
  options_value: string;
  // Deposits in transit, not yet settled.
  pending_deposits: string;
  // Total portfolio value across all asset types. The default 'account value' to show the user.
  total_value: string;
};
  // Show total_value as "account value" or "portfolio value". The breakdown fields show how value is distributed across asset types. Zero-value fields mean the user has no holdings in that asset class — omit them from display unless the user explicitly asks. For "how much cash do I have?" use the cash field. buying_power is the real-time authoritative spendable figure from the broker; quote buying_power.buying_power as the primary affordability answer with buying_power.display_currency. For "how much can I spend on crypto?" quote crypto_buying_power.buying_power instead — crypto is cash-only (not marginable), so the top-level buying_power may overstate it; crypto_buying_power is omitted when unavailable. crypto_value reflects the user's crypto holdings value. On a cash account, recently sold proceeds that have not yet settled are not spendable and are not included in buying_power; that unsettled amount is reported as unsettled_funds by get_accounts (not here), so if you do not already have the account's unsettled_funds, call get_accounts to check it. Whenever you report buying power or account value for a type='cash' account whose unsettled_funds is greater than 0, and the user wants to access unsettled funds or other features available in a limited margin account — if the get_limited_margin_upgrade_info tool is available — call it with the account_number and share the appropriate upgrade link. If that tool is not available, say nothing about limited margin (there is no fallback).
  guide: string;
}>>; };
